  "notes": "University Park. Scraped from bulletin archive 2022-2023. Total credits per bulletin: 124-129 (corrected 2026-09-18 audit -- the archived page's own printed total is 124-129, not 125-132 as previously stated here; this file's own item sum is 124, consistent). All course codes verified from linked course descriptions on bulletin page, except CAS 100 (unverified, but alternatives CAS 100A/B/C are verified). Note: This historical plan differs from VBS-2026 (manual optimizations added for prerequisite scheduling in 2026 version). 2026-09-18 audit fix: build_full_plan raised 'Could not schedule' for CHEM 110/111/112/113/202/203, STAT 250, BMB 401, MICRB 201/202 -- CHEM 110 has a real 'MATH 22' prereq (confirmed in the current course catalog), and MATH 22 itself needs MATH 21, neither of which this scraped plan ever included, so the whole downstream chemistry/biochem sequence was silently unschedulable (the engine dropped those requirements instead of raising an error, and previous self-verification wrongly reported 'goal met'). Added MATH 21 (Semester 1, no real prereq of its own) and MATH 22 (Semester 2) to unlock CHEM 110, and CHEM 130 (Semester 3, needs only MATH 21) as a faster alternate route into CHEM 202 so the BMB 211/212/221 track doesn't have to wait on the full CHEM 110->112->210/212 sequence -- same fix already applied in VBS-2026. Re-verified: 0 'Could not schedule' warnings. Residual, honestly-disclosed limitation: build_full_plan from a from-scratch simulation (zero prior credit) now needs 9 terms (one beyond the standard 4-year/8-term window) because MATH 21->22 must happen before CHEM 110 can start, delaying the whole chemistry sequence by roughly a semester; a real student who places directly into MATH 140 (the common case for this major) already has MATH 21/22 waived under this codebase's own math_placement.py ALEKS-ladder logic and would not see this delay in practice, but build_full_plan's term-by-term simulation does not apply that placement waiver to courses it schedules mid-simulation, only to a student's already-completed courses passed in up front.",
